diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index 32151de5..8b4d26bc 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -39,7 +39,7 @@ Here’s what the QMX+ PanadapterShared adds on top of upstream QMX-Panadapter,
 * No more freeze after boot: a network freeze about 20–50 s after boot, caused by the time update, is fixed.
 
 **Mac tools**
-* TCI bridge for the Mac (tools/mac-serial-bridge): WS (formerly WSJT-X Improved), WSJT-X 3 and JTDX use the NAS-shared QMX as a TCI network radio, with receive audio (from the I/Q), CAT and transmit (FT8/FT4/JS8/WSPR tones) in one connection; no serial port or virtual audio device. The Tab5 keeps its own connection. Bench-tested against the relay's fake radio; not yet tested with WS on the air.
+* TCI bridge for the Mac (tools/mac-serial-bridge): WS (formerly WSJT-X Improved), WSJT-X 3 and JTDX use the NAS-shared QMX as a TCI network radio, with receive audio (from the I/Q), CAT and transmit (FT8/FT4/JS8/WSPR tones) in one connection; no serial port or virtual audio device. The Tab5 keeps its own connection; while a TCI program is connected the relay keeps the Tab5 off the transmitter (and the Mac off while the Tab5 transmits). Bench-tested against the relay's fake radio; not yet tested with WS on the air.
 * Build and flash from a Mac (tools/mac-build-flash.sh), and record the Tab5’s serial log (tools/mac-capture-serial.sh).
 
 **Keeping up with upstream**
diff --git a/tools/mac-serial-bridge/README.md b/tools/mac-serial-bridge/README.md
index d5eac2f6..93a839e6 100644
--- a/tools/mac-serial-bridge/README.md
+++ b/tools/mac-serial-bridge/README.md
@@ -23,8 +23,11 @@ QMX ──USB──▶ NAS relay ──TCP 7357 (copy of the I/Q, listen only)
   as `TA<Hz>;` - the same way the Tab5 transmits - on a fixed 50 ms delay so
   every symbol keeps its spacing (bench test: 160 ms ± 1 ms, within 0.2 Hz).
   **Voice (SSB) does not work**: the QMX has no transmit-audio path over the network.
-* The Tab5 keeps its own connection and is not affected: it can stay on the
-  panadapter while WS runs on the Mac. Don't let both transmit at once.
+* The Tab5 keeps its own connection: it can stay on the panadapter while WS
+  runs on the Mac. While WS is connected the relay keeps the Tab5 off the
+  transmitter (its transmit commands are dropped), and while the Tab5 is
+  transmitting it keeps the Mac off. A file named `tab5-tx-always` in the
+  relay folder lets the Tab5 transmit anyway.
 
 The bridge's older mode, a serial port at `~/cu.QMX-NAS` (CAT only), is still
 there (`MODE=serial bash install-mac.sh`), but WS and WSJT-X could not open that
@@ -102,4 +105,4 @@ bridge leaves to the Tab5. It uses the relay's port 3 service on the same NAS,
   digital modes use.
 * One TCI program at a time; a second one takes over from the first. One PC at
   a time on QMX port 3 (a PC program on TCP 7356 and the bridge share it).
-* The QMX can't transmit for the Tab5 and for WS at the same time.
+* One transmitter at a time: while WS is connected the Tab5 can't transmit (see above).
